	// inner pages: copy from copy-inner.json (patterns/page-*.php)
	'about_title' => 'About',
	'about_intro' => 'Founded in 2019 in Ghent, Mame Coffee is a small roastery and coffee bar dedicated to sourcing, roasting, and serving a disciplined selection of coffees. We work with only a handful of producer relationships each season.',
	'about_h1' => 'Why do we buy and roast so few lots?',
	'about_b1' => 'We deliberately choose to buy only six green lots across the entire year, keeping just four active on the bar at any given time. Rather than maintaining a sprawling catalogue where bags sit in storage for months, we commit deeply to coffees we know intimately. Every lot is chosen directly at origin by our green buyer, Tomas Varga, who evaluates cherry ripeness and drying practices on site. Focusing on a small rotation allows our roasting team to understand each coffee\'s precise thermodynamic behavior in our 5 kg drum roaster. It also means that when you order a filter coffee at our Ghent bar, you are experiencing a lot roasted this week at peak sweetness, rather than an afterthought from a warehouse shelf.',
	'about_h2' => 'How did Mame Coffee begin in 2019?',
	'about_b2' => 'Mame Coffee was founded in 2019 by head roaster Ines Duarte and green buyer Tomas Varga after years spent roasting and sourcing coffee for other operations across Europe. Having worked within industrial volumes, both wanted to return to a calm, human scale where craft and origin traceability could remain paramount. They established our permanent roastery and bar at Branderijstraat 12 in Ghent, installing a single 5 kg drum roaster in the main hall. The space was designed so that the boundary between roasting and hospitality remains open. Guests sitting at the timber counter can observe green jute sacks being opened, production batches dropping from the drum door, and quality cuppings taking place throughout the week.',
	'about_h3' => 'Why do we pour coffee only between day 5 and day 28?',
	'about_b3' => 'Specialty coffee requires time to degas after roasting, but its vibrant aromatics and delicate organic acids begin to oxidize and flatten within a month. At Mame Coffee, we strictly serve and sell coffee between day 5 and day 28 following its roast date. Days one through four allow volatile carbon dioxide to dissipate from the cellular structure of the bean, preventing sour or harsh extraction notes in the cup. From day 5 onward, sweetness and floral definition reach their peak clarity. By day 28, any remaining roasted coffee is removed from our bar service. This disciplined twenty-four day window ensures that whether you drink a pour-over at our counter or take home a bag, every cup expresses its true harvest character.',
	'lots_title' => 'Current Lots',
	'lots_intro' => 'We roast four distinct coffees on our 5 kg drum roaster, chosen directly from harvest and poured exclusively between day 5 and day 28. Every lot is roasted on Monday and Tuesday mornings in Ghent.',
	'lots_h1' => 'What coffees are currently available on the bar?',
	'lots_b1' => 'Our current bar rotation features four distinct origins, roasted fresh every Monday and Tuesday. Kiamara from Nyeri, Kenya is a washed lot grown at 1,800 meters, rated sweetness 3, acidity 5, and body 3, priced at €17 per 200 g bag. Alto Rocío from Huila, Colombia is a washed lot cultivated at 1,700 meters, showing sweetness 4, acidity 3, and body 3, priced at €15 per 200 g bag. Hara Bura from Guji, Ethiopia is a natural lot produced at 2,000 meters, scoring sweetness 5, acidity 3, and body 2, available for €16 per 200 g bag. Finally, El Mirador from Chalatenango, El Salvador is a honey processed coffee from 1,600 meters with sweetness 4, acidity 2, and body 4, priced at €18 per 200 g bag.',
	'lots_h2' => 'What coffees were featured in our past lots?',
	'lots_b2' => 'Over the 2025 and 2026 seasons, our bar has featured six finished lots that have now run their course. These included San Antonio from Colombia, a washed lot that stayed on our bar for three months; Kirinyaga Peaberry from Kenya, a washed harvest served across twelve weeks; and Yaye from Ethiopia, an intense natural lot roasted throughout late autumn. We also presented La Cabaña from Colombia, processed as a honey lot and poured for ten weeks; Chelchele from Ethiopia, a washed selection active for two months; and Santa Rosa from El Salvador, a washed crop served during the winter rotation. Once a lot is depleted, we do not restock it until the next harvest cycle.',
	'lots_h3' => 'How can you taste and purchase our roasted beans?',
	'lots_b3' => 'Guests are welcome to taste all four current offerings before buying during our public cupping every Saturday from 10:00 to 11:00 at our roastery bar. If you cannot attend a cupping session, our baristas are always ready to pour any current lot as a filter brew at the counter. Roasted beans are packaged in 200 g bags and can be purchased directly at our bar at Branderijstraat 12. If you are located outside Ghent or wish to secure a specific roast day, you can also ask for bags by sending an email to hello@mamecoffee.example. We roast every Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday morning, packing orders immediately for pickup or dispatch.',
	'bar_title_page' => 'Bar Menu',
	'bar_intro' => 'Our coffee bar at Branderijstraat 12 is open Wednesday through Sunday from 8:00 to 16:00, serving freshly roasted coffee alongside simple baked goods. On Monday and Tuesday, our doors remain closed to guests while we roast.',
	'bar_h1' => 'What drinks and pastries are served at the bar?',
	'bar_b1' => 'The beverage menu at our bar reflects our four current seasonal lots. We serve Filter coffee brewed from the current lot for €4.50, and single Espresso pulled on our counter for €3.00. For milk-based drinks, we prepare Cortado for €3.80 and Flat white for €4.20, each balanced to preserve the inherent roast character. Guests wishing to compare origins side by side can order our Pour-over flight, featuring two distinct lots served in 150 ml carafes for €8.00. During warm summer months, we also pour Cold brew prepared from the current seasonal lot for €4.50. To accompany your coffee, we bake fresh Cardamom buns every morning, priced at €3.50.',
	'bar_h2' => 'Which whole bean bags are available to take home?',
	'bar_b2' => 'All coffees featured on our brew bar can be taken home in sealed 200 g bags, each stamped with its exact roast date. We offer Kiamara from Nyeri, Kenya at €17, displaying vibrant blackcurrant acidity and citrus brightness. Alto Rocío from Huila, Colombia is available for €15, providing sweet stone fruit and clean caramel tones. Hara Bura from Guji, Ethiopia costs €16, celebrated for its floral strawberry sweetness and delicate tea-like body. El Mirador from Chalatenango, El Salvador is offered at €18, delivering rich honey sweetness and a velvety chocolate finish. Our team will gladly grind whole beans to match your specific brewing equipment upon request.',
	'bar_h3' => 'Who leads the service behind our coffee counter?',
	'bar_b3' => 'Daily counter service and beverage quality are directed by our head barista, Lena Brandt. Lena joined Mame Coffee to establish a calm, unhurried hospitality style that gives every guest the opportunity to ask questions about roast profiles, extraction parameters, and origin stories. Working alongside barista Rafael Souza, Lena monitors water composition, grind settings, and brew temperatures multiple times each day to ensure every cup pours precisely to recipe. Whether you are stopping by for a morning espresso or settling in for an afternoon pour-over flight, Lena and our team ensure your coffee is poured between day 5 and day 28 of resting.',
	'roastery_title' => 'The Roastery',
	'roastery_intro' => 'Our entire production takes place inside our hall at Branderijstraat 12 on a single 5 kg drum roaster. We roast three mornings a week, prioritizing clarity, sweetness, and consistent batch development.',
	'roastery_h1' => 'How does our 5 kg drum roaster shape our coffee?',
	'roastery_b1' => 'At the heart of Mame Coffee is a single 5 kg drum roaster, chosen specifically because its small capacity permits exacting thermal control over every batch. Head roaster Ines Duarte and roaster Amara Okoye manage the burner pressure, airflow, and drum speed manually, adjusting energy input to match the moisture content and bean density of each origin. Rather than relying on automated factory curves, we roast in small 5 kg charges three mornings a week, starting at 6:30. This deliberate batch size ensures that heat distributes evenly across every bean, highlighting terroir and preserving floral acids without introducing scorched or baked roast defects.',
	'roastery_h2' => 'What happens when green coffee arrives at the roastery?',
	'roastery_b2' => 'Production begins long before coffee enters the hot drum. When green coffee arrives in jute sacks at our Ghent hall, we unpack the bags and inspect the raw seeds by hand. Using round sieves, our team checks screen size uniformity and removes any foreign matter or defective seeds that slipped past origin dry mills. We measure green bean moisture and water activity before executing sample roasts on small test batches. Tomas Varga, Ines Duarte, and Mei Lin Ho cup these sample roasts together to calibrate our target development time and end temperature before committing any lot to our production schedule.',
	'roastery_h3' => 'How do production roasting and resting cycles work?',
	'roastery_b3' => 'Production roasting takes place on Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday mornings starting at 6:30. Ines Duarte roasts our Kiamara and Alto Rocío lots on Mondays, while Amara Okoye roasts Hara Bura and El Mirador on Tuesdays. On Thursday mornings, Tomas Varga steps up to roast whichever lot is running lowest on the bar. Every Thursday at 11:00, Mei Lin Ho cups every production batch roasted during the week to confirm sweetness and extraction balance. Once roasted beans drop into the round cooling tray, they rest until day 5 before being ground, and are completely served by day 28.',
	'origins_title' => 'Our Origins',
	'origins_intro' => 'We source our green coffee through direct, long-standing relationships with coffee producers across four growing regions. Tomas Varga selects only six green lots per year, visiting farms during harvest to evaluate cultivation and drying.',
	'origins_h1' => 'How does Tomas Varga select our green coffee lots?',
	'origins_b1' => 'Green buying at Mame Coffee is directed by Tomas Varga, who travels to origin farms during harvest season to evaluate growing conditions firsthand. Tomas spends time walking the slopes, inspecting tree health, and talking with growers beside raised drying beds and washing stations. We commit to buying only six green lots each year, allowing us to build genuine commercial continuity with our partner producers rather than trading opportunistically on the spot market. Tomas evaluates cup clarity, structural sweetness, and regional typicity, selecting harvests that perform exceptionally on our 5 kg drum roaster without requiring artificial processing tricks or exaggerated fermentations.',
	'origins_h2' => 'What characterizes our East African partner farms?',
	'origins_b2' => 'Our East African offerings represent two historic growing traditions. In Kenya, our Kiamara lot comes from the high red volcanic soils of Nyeri, cultivated at 1,800 meters above sea level. This lot undergoes classical double washed processing with clean river water, producing unmatched structural acidity and intense blackcurrant sweetness. In Ethiopia, our Hara Bura lot originates in the Guji zone at an elevation of 2,000 meters. Processed as a traditional natural coffee, ripe cherries are dried slowly on raised African beds under partial shade, yielding a cup with layered strawberry aromatics, gentle tea-like body, and profound fruit sweetness.',
	'origins_h3' => 'What defines our Latin American producer lots?',
	'origins_b3' => 'Our Latin American coffees showcase meticulous washing and honey processing. In Colombia, our Alto Rocío lot is cultivated at 1,700 meters in the rugged mountains of Huila. Grown under native shade trees, the cherries are pulped and wet fermented before undergoing sun drying on covered patios, producing a balanced cup characterized by juicy stone fruit, crisp malic acidity, and cane sugar sweetness. In El Salvador, our El Mirador lot is grown at 1,600 meters in the Chalatenango department. Prepared using honey processing where mucilage remains on the seed during drying, it yields rounded milk chocolate sweetness and creamy body.',
	'people_title' => 'People',
	'people_intro' => 'Mame Coffee is operated by a small team of seven people dedicated to green sourcing, drum roasting, and bar hospitality. We work together at Branderijstraat 12 in Ghent to bring calm precision to every step.',
	'people_h1' => 'Who founded Mame Coffee and leads our operations?',
	'people_b1' => 'Mame Coffee was established in 2019 by co-founders Ines Duarte and Tomas Varga. Ines serves as head roaster, bringing over a decade of technical drum roasting experience to our Ghent facility. She oversees roasting profiles, machine maintenance, and roast schedules every week. Tomas Varga acts as our green coffee buyer, traveling to origin to secure our six annual harvests and maintaining direct relationships with producers. Front of house service is anchored by Lena Brandt, our head barista. Lena manages daily bar workflow, trains staff on extraction parameters, and ensures that hospitality at the counter remains warm, calm, and attentive.',
	'people_h2' => 'Who manages roasting schedules and quality control?',
	'people_b2' => 'Roasting and quality evaluation involve shared responsibility across our team. Amara Okoye roasts every Tuesday morning, taking charge of our Hara Bura natural lot from Ethiopia and El Mirador honey lot from El Salvador on the 5 kg drum. Tomas Varga roasts on Thursday mornings to replenish whichever lot is running lowest on inventory. Quality control is led by Mei Lin Ho, who oversees our cupping lab. Every Thursday at 11:00, Mei Lin sets up evaluation bowls for all batches roasted during the week, assessing sweetness, extraction consistency, and degassing progress before lots are approved for service.',
	'people_h3' => 'Who oversees brewing classes, bar service, and wholesale?',
	'people_b3' => 'Behind the espresso machine and out on the road, our operations rely on Rafael Souza and Joost de Wit. Rafael is a skilled barista who leads daily counter service and hosts our monthly home brew classes on the first Sunday of each month. Joost de Wit directs wholesale relations and regional logistics, personally delivering freshly roasted beans to our Ghent cafe partners by cargo bike every Thursday. Together with Rafael, Joost also visits wholesale partners to provide technical machinery calibration and grinder maintenance, ensuring our coffees are extracted properly beyond our own roastery bar.',
	'cupping_title' => 'Cupping & Classes',
	'cupping_intro' => 'We host regular public coffee cuppings and home brewing classes at our roastery hall in Ghent. All sessions are kept small to allow for direct guidance around the cupping table.',
	'cupping_h1' => 'What happens during our Saturday public cupping?',
	'cupping_b1' => 'Every Saturday from 10:00 to 11:00, we open our cupping table to the public at Branderijstraat 12. Priced at €12 per person and limited strictly to 8 seats, this one-hour session allows guests to taste our four current bar lots alongside sample roasts and development batches. Guided by our coffee team, participants learn standard industry cupping etiquette: evaluating dry fragrance, assessing wet aroma after crust breaking, and slurping from deep spoons to analyze sweetness, acidity, and body. It is an unhurried, informal environment where you can explore how different origins and processing methods compare side by side.',
	'cupping_h2' => 'What is taught in our monthly home brew class?',
	'cupping_b2' => 'Held on the first Sunday of every month from 14:00 to 16:00, our home brew class costs €45 and is capped at 6 seats. Taught by barista Rafael Souza, this practical workshop covers pour-over mechanics, grinder burr calibration, and water temperature dynamics. Rafael demonstrates our house recipe: 15 g of medium-ground coffee, 250 g of soft water heated to 93 °C, an initial 40-second bloom, followed by three steady concentric pours finishing precisely at 3:00. Participants practice hands-on pouring technique, learning how flow rate and agitation influence extraction clarity and sweetness in their home brewing.',
	'cupping_h3' => 'How do you book a cupping seat or class reservation?',
	'cupping_b3' => 'Because capacity is strictly limited to 8 guests for Saturday cupping and 6 guests for Sunday brewing classes, advance reservation is required. We do not use third-party ticketing platforms or complicated online forms; simply send an email directly to hello@mamecoffee.example stating your preferred date, class type, and number of attendees. We will confirm your seat within twenty-four hours and provide instructions for payment. All sessions take place around the central timber table inside our Ghent roastery at Branderijstraat 12, with freshly roasted coffee beans and tasting notes included in the booking.',
	'wholesale_title' => 'Wholesale Coffee',
	'wholesale_intro' => 'We supply specialty coffee bars, bakeries, and quality-focused workplaces across Belgium and neighboring European regions with freshly roasted coffee. We roast to order three mornings a week so that every lot reaches our partners ready for service.',
	'wholesale_h1' => 'What are the minimum orders and roast schedules for wholesale?',
	'wholesale_b1' => 'We work with wholesale partners who share our focus on cup quality and thoughtful service. Our wholesale program requires a modest minimum order of 5 kg per month, making it accessible to independent cafes, design studios, and culinary establishments. All wholesale orders are roasted to order on our 5 kg drum roaster during our regular production mornings on Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday. Beans are packaged in sealed valved bags with clear roast dates, ensuring your espresso hopper and filter stations receive coffee that reaches its optimal extraction window between day 5 and day 28.',
	'wholesale_h2' => 'How are wholesale deliveries handled in Ghent and beyond?',
	'wholesale_b2' => 'We take responsibility for the final leg of our supply chain. For wholesale partners located within Ghent, Joost de Wit delivers freshly roasted coffee directly to your door by cargo bicycle every Thursday afternoon, eliminating packaging waste and motor emissions. For cafes and restaurants outside Ghent across Belgium and neighboring European regions, orders are dispatched every Thursday via courier service for next-day arrival. Because we coordinate delivery immediately following our Thursday production roasts, wholesale accounts receive their beans with sufficient resting time to enter service by the following week.',
	'wholesale_h3' => 'What technical support and equipment advice do we provide?',
	'wholesale_b3' => 'A great harvest and careful roast can only succeed if extraction equipment is dialed in correctly. Our team members Rafael Souza and Joost de Wit offer hands-on technical guidance to all wholesale accounts. We assist with espresso machine water filtration, grinder burr alignment, volumetric recipe setup, and barista workflow training. If you are planning a new bar build or upgrading existing brewing machinery, Rafael and Joost can advise on hardware selection suited to your volume. To inquire about opening a wholesale account, contact us at hello@mamecoffee.example.',
	'journal_title' => 'Journal',
	'journal_intro' => 'Our journal documents field trips to origin farms, roastery observations, extraction techniques, and seasonal reflections from our team in Ghent. Here we share notes on the work behind our coffees.',
	'journal_h1' => 'What stories emerge from our origin journeys?',
	'journal_b1' => 'Our origin essays recount the people, landscapes, and harvest rhythms behind our six annual coffee lots. In our field report \'A week at Hara Bura\', green buyer Tomas Varga recounts his journey into the highlands of Guji, Ethiopia, where coffee cherries dry under intense equatorial sunlight on raised timber beds. Sitting with farming families and walking the mist-laden hillside plots reveals why natural processed coffees require such painstaking sorting and moisture management. These personal dispatches connect the quiet glass of filter coffee poured at our counter with the soil, hands, and weather that shaped it.',
	'journal_h2' => 'How do roastery craft and hand sorting ensure quality?',
	'journal_b2' => 'Behind the roaster drum, quiet craftsmanship determines the consistency of our daily output. In \'Sorting by hand\', we examine the tactile labor of running green coffee through round sieves, inspecting raw seeds before they enter the roaster to eliminate physical imperfections. In \'Day 5 to day 28\', head roaster Ines Duarte outlines the chemical resting curve of our beans, explaining why freshly roasted coffee needs four days of carbon dioxide degassing before revealing its true sweetness on day 5, and why every batch must be enjoyed before day 28.',
	'journal_h3' => 'How has Mame Coffee evolved since our founding in 2019?',
	'journal_b3' => 'Looking back to our beginnings in Ghent, our essay \'Our first lot, 2019\' reflects on the principles established when Ines Duarte and Tomas Varga opened our doors at Branderijstraat 12. Starting with a single 5 kg drum roaster and a quiet conviction to serve only coffees we understood completely, our roastery has grown into a tight-knit team of seven. While seasonal harvests come and go, our dedication to buying few lots, roasting three mornings a week, and serving coffees at peak clarity remains identical to our very first morning of service.',
	'visit_title' => 'Visit Us',
	'visit_intro' => 'Our roastery hall and coffee bar are located at Branderijstraat 12 in Ghent, welcoming guests Wednesday through Sunday from 8:00 to 16:00. On Monday and Tuesday, our space is reserved for roasting.',
	'visit_h1' => 'Where is our roastery located and how can you reach us?',
	'visit_b1' => 'Mame Coffee is situated at Branderijstraat 12, 9000 Ghent, Belgium. Our roastery and bar are located just five minutes\' walk from Gent-Dampoort railway station, making us easily reachable by train, bicycle, or on foot from the historic city center. We occupy an airy industrial hall with tall steel-framed windows where morning light illuminates the production space. For questions regarding accessibility, group visits, or bean availability, you can telephone us at +32 9 000 12 34 or email our team at hello@mamecoffee.example. Bicycle parking is readily available directly outside our front entrance.',
	'visit_h2' => 'What are our opening hours and roasting schedule?',
	'visit_b2' => 'Our coffee bar welcomes guests five days a week, opening Wednesday through Sunday from 8:00 to 16:00. On Mondays and Tuesdays, our doors are closed to the public so our roasting team can focus fully on green sorting, sample evaluation, and production batches. Production roasting runs on Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday mornings starting promptly at 6:30. When you visit our bar on Thursday mornings, you can sit at the timber counter and watch Ines Duarte, Amara Okoye, or Tomas Varga tending the 5 kg drum roaster as fresh beans drop into the cooling tray.',
	'visit_h3' => 'What should you expect when you visit our bar?',
	'visit_b3' => 'When you step inside our hall, ask for Lena Brandt at the bar. Lena and barista Rafael Souza will guide you through our four seasonal lots, poured as single origin filter, espresso, or comparative pour-over flights. You can take a seat along the counter while the roaster cools, enjoy a freshly baked cardamom bun for €3.50, and pick up whole bean bags roasted within the month. If you visit on a Saturday morning between 10:00 and 11:00, you can also join our weekly public cupping table to taste fresh roasts beside our production crew.',

